    "title": "Tangent Lines on a Circle via Implicit Differentiation",
    "caption_md": "The circle $x^2 + y^2 = 25$ cannot be written as a single function $y = f(x)$, yet implicit differentiation gives us $\\frac{dy}{dx} = -\\frac{x}{y}$ at every point. Two tangent lines are shown: one at $(3, 4)$ with slope $-\\frac{3}{4}$, and one at $(-4, 3)$ with slope $\\frac{4}{3}$. Notice that each slope depends on **both** $x$ and $y$ — the same $x$-value can yield different slopes on the upper and lower halves of the circle. Verify each slope by plugging the point into $-x/y$.",
